cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Cite the paper for the formal result and the evidence archive when referring to the recorded mainnet execution."
title: "Aspis: End-to-End Formal Verification of a Transparent Private Spend on Solana"
type: software
version: "5"
date-released: 2026-08-25
authors:
  - family-names: "Barker"
    given-names: "Dominic"
    website: "https://github.com/DJBarker87"
abstract: >-
  To our knowledge at the 24 August 2026 public-evidence cutoff, Aspis provides
  the first publicly evidenced Solana mainnet transaction to directly verify a
  transparent, computationally hiding private-spend proof and atomically
  record its nullifier and new pool state. The transaction verified a
  75,358-byte proof in 1,334,452 compute units. Aspis also provides an
  end-to-end Lean theorem for every successful execution of the deployed proof
  checker. From one translated Rust call, the theorem derives the parse,
  Fiat-Shamir transcript, six work checks, 18 distinct queries, five
  authenticated opening sections, four low-degree folds, final polynomial,
  complete algebraic relation, and both final accumulators. The mathematical
  development proves spend-witness extraction, the concrete field and encoder
  results, coherent four-fold extraction, the exact query-sampler law, fold
  duality, theft and state reductions, and the finite failure decomposition.
  The 331-module replay is paired with pinned translation inputs, a
  byte-reproducible 1,258,496-byte program, and the finalized execution
  archive. The paper gives a conditional work-normalized security bound and
  reports raw post-grinding accounting separately.
